# Split — content and a rail. Two columns where the second is NOT a half: the
# content column takes the space and the rail takes what it needs (320px), which
# is what a sidebar actually is. `Split3` is the same idea with a nav rail on the
# left as well.
#
#     use Split():
#         use Stack():
#             ...the page...
#         use Sticky():
#             use Panel...
#
# Exactly two children, in order. That is a convention this language cannot check
# — a `slot` is one hole, not two typed sockets, and the thing that CAN check it
# is a `wireframe`, whose `socket nav: ui` is the typed version of this and is
# what `wireframes/shell.fct` is built from. So `Split` is the lightweight form
# for a plain app, and a build that wants the arrangement enforced uses a
# wireframe instead. Passing three children is not an error; it is a third column
# 320px wide, which is visible immediately.
#
# `Sticky` is the rail's companion, separate rather than built in, because a rail
# that always sticks is wrong as often as it is right: a short rail should stick,
# and a rail longer than the viewport that sticks is a rail whose bottom can never
# be reached.
#
# `minmax(0,1fr)` on the content column — in vocabulary.fct — is what stops one
# long unbroken word from widening the grid and pushing the rail off the screen.
# Below 1100px both splits become one column, the rail falling under the content.
import "vocabulary.fct"
app FacetLayout:
    component Split():
        box class "x-l-split":
            slot
    component Split3():
        box class "x-l-split-3":
            slot
    component Sticky():
        box class "x-l-sticky":
            slot
